{
  "dom_id": "step-q327668",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Given that the expression $6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+\u03d5)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+\u03d5) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x+\u03d5),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5=6 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\u03d5)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\u03d5)}^{2} & ={6}^{2}+{8}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}\u2061\u03d5+{sin}^{2}\u2061\u03d5) & =36+64 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =100 \\\\ R & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$",
